mov: option to index the fragments of a seekable file lazily

A fragmented file without a complete sidx is indexed while it opens: on a
seekable input the header reads every top-level moof to the end of the
file, one seek per fragment, before it returns. Over a network that is a
request per fragment ahead of the first packet. Measured on a 4 minute,
61 MB file of 96 fragments on a WAN address (35 ms round trip): 12 s and
about 90 requests to open, and the same again for the first seek.

lazy_frag_index (off by default) stops the header behind the first moof
and leaves the rest to the seeks. A seek estimates a byte offset from the
fragments it knows, resynchronises on the next moof, reads its tfdt and
bisects on that until it has the fragment that holds the target. A
resynchronised moof counts only when it parses in full (box sizes that add
up, mfhd first, track ids the moov declares, a tfdt for every track with
samples, sample data inside the mdat behind it) and the box chain behind
it leads to another fragment or to the end of the media, so the estimate
decides how many reads a seek takes and never where it lands. When the
file has an mfra its moof offsets are used as hints, each checked against
the moof it points at; its times are never used for timestamps. A complete
sidx keeps the existing path.

The sample is then looked up in the stream index as before. So that it is
the sample a complete index gives, the seek also reads the headers of the
neighbouring fragments where that sample can be in one of them: backwards
to a sync sample when the fragment does not start with one, forwards for a
forward seek, and backwards for the other audio and video streams, whose
sample at the seek time a muxer may have put in an earlier fragment.

A stream that follows the seeked one, and a seeked stream that is not the
reference track, also reads the fragments after the one the seek resolved
to, as far as its sample can be there: forward to the fragment that holds
it, so that a video stream starts on a sync sample when fragments do not
start with one, and backward while the next fragment starts the stream at
or before the seek time.

The streams get their durations when the file opens, from the last
fragment of each track: through the mfra when there is one, else by
resynchronising near the end of the file. A file is read as before, every
moof, when its fragments carry no tfdt (their time is then only known by
adding up everything before them), when its end cannot be found that way
(a truncated file), or when the input is not seekable.

Differences from a full index that remain with the option on:
- the per-stream bit rate is not set at open (it was the total sample size
  over the duration; one fragment says nothing about it), the container
  bit rate is unchanged;
- a file with a separate moof per track delivers the same packets with the
  same timestamps in file order, a video fragment and then the audio
  fragment behind it, rather than interleaved by timestamp across them;
- boxes behind the last fragment other than the mfra are not read;
- a forward seek past the last sync sample of a stream leaves the streams
  that follow the seeked one at their end; a full index leaves them where
  they were.

Measured on the file above over the same WAN address: 0.9 s and 4 requests
to open instead of 12 s and about 90, identical stream durations, and 30
seeks that deliver packet for packet what the full index delivers. Checked
packet for packet against the unpatched demuxer on 18 fragmented layouts
(absolute and moof-relative offsets, with and without mfra, sidx per
fragment and global, fragments that do not start with a sync sample,
separate moofs per track, negative composition offsets, edit lists with
empty and shifted edits, samples in the moov, audio only, truncated, no
tfdt), backward, forward and any-sample seeks on the video and the audio
stream, cold and in long sessions with reads in between. Without the
option, and with it on files that are not fragmented, packets and reads
are those of the unpatched demuxer.

